    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d701">

                <pb facs="0992" n="982" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg982"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.24/5–146: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Embassy in China
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-05-01T15:00:00-04:00">May 1, 1946—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>58. On April 4, Messrs. Acheson and Clayton handed following <hi
                        rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> to Chinese Ambassador here: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Department proposes negotiations begin at once in Washington on
                            settlement war account between US and China including settlement
                            lend-lease, surplus property, yuan advances, and other claims arising
                            out of war.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Important item for settlement lend-lease is inventory of lend-lease goods in
                    hands of Chinese civilian agencies on V–J Day. On August 19 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">18</hi>], 1945 Crowley, then FEA Administrator, asked in
                    letter to Dr. Wei Tao-Ming, Chinese Supply Commission here,<anchor n="23"
                        xml:id="d701-f1"/> “that you furnish as soon as possible an inventory
                    listing as of V–J Day all articles that have been transferred to your Government
                    by the FEA on lend-lease terms and that have not been lost, destroyed, or
                    consumed”. This letter also brought to attention Dr. Wong Wen-Hao, Chairman
                    Chinese WPB, in letter from William Stanton, FEA representative Chungking, on
                    November 27, 1945.</p>
                <p>No reply and no inventory report received to date from Chinese.</p>
                <p>Suggest urgently you tell Dr. Soong (and inform General Marshall) we want
                    inventory report earliest possible but certainly by time of negotiations; if not
                    made available by Chinese at that time, they must accept our estimates of
                    probable value of inventory.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
